# Spec — Compendium lenses (WS2) + travel-graph wiring (WS3)

Repo: `veydria-cartography`. You are implementing in an isolated worktree. The
map app mirrors canonical data from the upstream `worldbuilder` repo. Two
independent units of work below. **Do NOT touch any file outside the lists name
d
here. Match the surrounding code style exactly. Add tests for every new behavio
r.**

Design decisions are already made (below) — implement them as written; do not
redesign. Where a choice is left open it says so explicitly.

---

## WS2 — Four new compendium browse lenses

The compendium browses canon entities through a fixed lens list. A recent canon
refresh added four content domains that currently have NO browse lens (they are
only reachable via search). Add a lens for each.

### Files
- `web/src/components/compendium/types.ts` — the `LENSES: Lens[]` array.
- `web/src/components/CompendiumPanel.tsx` — the `switch (lens)` filter block
  (around lines 310–334, the `if (lens) { ... }` branch).
- A test file — extend an existing one near `web/src/utils/compendium-data.test
.ts`
  OR add `web/src/components/compendium/lenses.test.ts` (your call; follow the
  closest existing pattern).

### Exact lenses to add (append to the `LENSES` array, in this order, AFTER the
existing `figures` entry)

```
{ key: 'characters', label: 'Characters & Figures', description: 'Named persons
across the continent — journals, envoys, and recorded lives' },
{ key: 'record-keeping', label: 'Record-Keeping & Writing', description: 'Writi
ng media and record technologies — how each civilization commits word to materi
al' },
{ key: 'underclass', label: 'Underclass & Poverty', description: 'The asymmetri
c frame — bondage, debt, and the lives lived beneath the institutions' },
{ key: 'funerary', label: 'Death & Funerary Rites', description: 'Burial, mourn
ing, and the undecidable afterlife across the religion layer' },
```

### Exact filter cases to add to the `switch (lens)` block in CompendiumPanel.t
sx

```
case 'characters':
  filtered = entities.filter((e) => e.entity_type === 'character');
  break;
case 'record-keeping':
  filtered = entities.filter((e) => e.entity_type === 'record_medium');
  break;
case 'underclass':
  filtered = entities.filter((e) => e.entity_type === 'underclass_life');
  break;
case 'funerary':
  filtered = entities.filter((e) => e.entity_type === 'funerary_practice');
  break;
```

(These entity_type values are confirmed present in the refreshed
`web/public/canon.json`: `character`, `record_medium`, `underclass_life`,
`funerary_practice`.)

### Acceptance criteria (WS2)
- [ ] The four lens cards appear in the compendium lens grid, after "Named Figu
res".
- [ ] Selecting each lens filters to entities whose `entity_type` matches the t
able.
- [ ] A unit test asserts each new lens key filters a fixture entity set to exa
ctly
      the matching `entity_type` rows (mirror the existing lens/filter test sty
le).
- [ ] `npm run build` (tsc) and `npm run test` pass.
- [ ] No change to any lens that already existed.

---

## WS3 — Consume worldbuilder's authored travel-graph durations

Today the journey sim estimates every leg's duration **geometrically**
(`segmentDays = km / speed`, `web/src/utils/journey-graph.ts` ~line 633).
Worldbuilder ships `geography/continents/veydria-travel-graph.yaml` with
**Perry-ratified day-counts read from canon prose** for ~9 inter-place crossing
s
(sea sails, mountain passes, cliff descents). Wire those authored durations in:
where a route edge corresponds to an authored travel-graph edge, use the author
ed
day-count instead of the geometric estimate. Everything else stays geometric.

### Files
- `scripts/sync-world-data.mjs` — add ONE `SYNC_MAP` entry (see below).
- `web/src/utils/journey-graph.ts` — load authored durations; override `segment
Days`
  for matching edges; honor the Smith-Spring non-edge.
- `web/src/generated/veydria-travel-graph.json` — NEW generated file (the sync
  writes it; commit the generated output too, like `web/public/encounters.json`
).
- A new test: `web/src/utils/travel-graph-durations.test.ts` (or co-locate in
  `journey-graph.test.ts` — your call).

### Step 1 — Sync the file in (yaml → JSON, mirroring the encounters transform)
Add to `SYNC_MAP` in `sync-world-data.mjs`:
```
{
  src: 'geography/continents/veydria-travel-graph.yaml',
  dest: 'web/src/generated/veydria-travel-graph.json',
  description: 'Authored inter-place travel durations (ADR-0018); yaml→json for
the journey graph',
  optional: true,
  transform: 'yaml2json',
},
```
Then run `node scripts/sync-world-data.mjs` once and commit the generated
`web/src/generated/veydria-travel-graph.json`. (The `yaml2json` transform alrea
dy
exists in the script — reuse it; do not write a new parser.)

### Step 2 — A small typed loader + matcher in `journey-graph.ts`
Import the generated JSON synchronously (vite supports JSON imports; the calend
ar
uses the same `web/src/generated/` location):
```
import travelGraphData from '../generated/veydria-travel-graph.json'
```
Build, once at module load, a lookup from an **unordered, normalized node-id pa
ir**
to an authored day-count:
- Normalize an id: lowercase, replace `-` with `_`. Also index every node's
  `aliases` (from `travelGraphData.nodes.aliases`) to its canonical id so that
  hyphenated viewer slugs resolve.
- For each entry in `travelGraphData.edges`: key = sorted pair of the two
  normalized `between` ids; value = the authored days. `days` is either a numbe
r
  OR `{ min, max }` — for a range use the **midpoint** `(min + max) / 2`.
- Expose a helper `authoredDaysForEdge(fromId, toId): number | null` that retur
ns
  the authored days for the normalized unordered pair, or `null` if none.

### Step 3 — Override `segmentDays` for matching route edges
In `journey-graph.ts`, in the per-edge day-estimate loop (currently
`for (const edge of pathEdges) { ... edge.segmentDays = km / speed }`), change 
to:
```
for (const edge of pathEdges) {
  const authored = authoredDaysForEdge(edge.from, edge.to)
  if (authored != null) {
    // Canon-attested duration at the DEFAULT party (normal/foot/medium). Party
    // config still scales it via the same pace multiplier the geometric path u
ses,
    // so a faster party still arrives sooner.
    edge.segmentDays = authored / getPaceMultiplier(party, edge.type)
  } else {
    const km = svgDistanceToKm(edge.distanceSvg)
    const baseSpeed = speedByType || 25
    const speed = baseSpeed * getPaceMultiplier(party, edge.type)
    edge.segmentDays = km / speed
  }
}
```
Do NOT change `estimatedDays` — it already sums `segmentDays`, so it stays
consistent automatically. Do NOT mutate shared graph edges (the loop already
operates on cloned `pathEdges` — keep it that way).

### Step 4 — Honor the Smith-Spring intentional non-edge
The travel-graph `gaps:` records `between:  via: smith_spring
kind: turn_back_hub` — canon **forbids** that traverse. Ensure the pathfinder c
an
never return a route containing a direct `ngaru_bon ↔ irrah` edge:
- First DETERMINE whether the GeoJSON-built graph actually creates such a direc
t
  edge (run `npm run sim:journey` / inspect `buildGraph`). 
- If it does: suppress it — in `buildGraph` (`scripts/sim/run-journey.ts` and/o
r the
  web `buildGraph` in `journey-graph.ts`, wherever edges are added), skip addin
g any
  edge whose normalized endpoint pair is a travel-graph `gaps` entry with
  `kind: turn_back_hub`. Read the forbidden pairs from the same generated JSON
  (`travelGraphData.gaps` filtered to `kind === 'turn_back_hub'`).
- If it does NOT exist: add only a test asserting no direct `ngaru_bon↔irrah` e
dge
  is ever produced, and a one-line code comment noting the non-edge is honored 
by
  construction. Do not invent a suppression for an edge that cannot occur.
- **Report in your output which case held.**

### Scope honesty (state this, do not silently exceed)
Authored override applies ONLY to route edges whose BOTH endpoints are
travel-graph nodes (direct civ↔civ / port↔port / region↔sea legs). If the
pathfinder splits a crossing through an intermediate snapped oasis, those
sub-edges keep the geometric estimate. That is acceptable for this increment —
do not attempt to re-aggregate split legs.

### Acceptance criteria (WS3)
- [ ] `node scripts/sync-world-data.mjs --check` lists the new travel-graph ent
ry
      as in-sync; `web/src/generated/veydria-travel-graph.json` is committed.
- [ ] `authoredDaysForEdge` resolves: `ngaru_bon↔kheshkai` → 4; `tavakh_qarat↔k
i_mbuhari`
      → 3; `oravan↔aethelian_basin` → 1; a `{min,max}` edge (`qollari↔ndjadi`) 
→ 5
      (midpoint of 4–6); unordered (swap from/to gives same result); alias form
      (`ngaru-bon`) resolves; an unknown pair → null. Unit-tested.
- [ ] On a route containing an authored leg, that leg's `segmentDays` derives f
rom
      the authored day-count (÷ pace multiplier), NOT from `km/speed`. Unit-tes
ted
      against a small constructed route or a known sim route.
- [ ] Smith-Spring: no route ever contains a direct `ngaru_bon↔irrah` edge
      (test). Your report states whether suppression was needed or it was absen
t
      by construction.
- [ ] `npm run build`, `npm run test` (all existing 856 tests still pass — the
      existing `journey-graph.test.ts` invariants "estimatedDays == sum of
      segmentDays" and chokepoint behavior MUST still hold), and `npm run sim:j
ourney`
      all green.

